\
ReadMe for Edaphic factors, successional status, and functional traits drive tree habitat associations in naturally regenerating tropical dry forests, Werden LK, Becknell JM, Powers JS. Functional Ecology. \
\
This readme file describes the data files and supplementary files accompanying the publication above. For any further queries and if you would like to use these data in a publication please contact Leland Werden (lwerden@gmail.com).\
\
************************************************************************************************************\
\
The following files are included: \
\
1) \'93SpeciesLevelTraits_WerdenEtAl2018_FunctionalEcology.csv\'94 \
\
This file contains species-level functional trait data for each of the 82 tropical dry forest trees species used in the study. These data were collected using standard methods (P\'e9rez-Harguindeguy et al. 2013) in the conservation areas where the study was conducted and were collated from multiple sources. The rows in this file are species-level mean trait values, and the columns are the measured functional traits. The trait names in the file are not abbreviated and are self-explanatory. The units are included in the labels. Missing values are indicated with NAs. \
\
Data were collated from the following sources: From (Powers & Tiffin 2010) we collated mean species-level values of specific leaf area (SLA; cm^2 g^-1), leaf area (cm^2), leaf dry matter content (LDMC; g g^-1), wood density (g cm^-3), foliar \uc0\u948 13C (\'89), foliar phosphorus (P; %), nitrogen (N; %), and carbon (C; %), leaf type (simple or compound) and habit (deciduous, semi-deciduous, evergreen). Additional trait data were gathered for mean seed mass (g; Smith-Martin et al. 2017), maximum adult tree height (m; J.S. Powers, unpublished), wood vessel diameter (mm) and xylem vessel density (# vessels cm-3; Werden et al. 2018). Finally, we considered species-level dispersal syndrome (animal, wind or gravity, both; Powers et al. 2009; Hilje et al. 2015).\
\
************************************************************************************************************\
\
2) \'93ForestInventoryData_WerdenEtAl2018_FunctionalEcology.csv\'94\
\
This file contains tropical dry forest inventory data collected by Powers et al. (2009) and Becknell and Powers (2014) for 84 20 x 50 m (0.1 ha) plots along a 5-100 year forest age gradient. These data were collected in Sector Santa Rosa in the \'c1rea de Conservaci\'f3n Guanacaste (10.848\'baN, 85.628\'baW), and Parque Nacional Palo Verde in the \'c1rea de Conservaci\'f3n Tempisque (10.358\'baN, 85.358\'baW). In each plot every live tree \uc0\u8805  10 cm DBH was measured (3433 trees) and identified to species (146 species in 49 families). The rows in this file are individual trees and the columns are the data collected for each tree.\
\
Variable names and column descriptions: \
\
	observationID		unique number assigned to individual stems for analysis purposes\
	plotID				plot number (84 - 20 x 50 m plots total) 	\
	forestType			Forest type defined by Powers et al. (2009). SROAK = Santa Rosa Oak-dominated forest, SRTDF = Santa Rosa tropical dry forest, PVTDF = Palo Verde tropical dry forest \
	treeTag			number assigned to tree in each plot (not sequential over all plots)\
	family				family of individual\
	genus				genus of individual\
	species			species of individual\
	multiStem			if individual was multi-stemmed (yes: > one stem, no: one stem) see Becknell and Powers (2014) for more details\
	stem1_DBH_cm		diameter at breast height for first stem in cm\
	stem2_DBH_cm		diameter at breast height for second stem in cm (NA indicates not present)\
	stem3_DBH_cm		diameter at breast height for third stem in cm (NA indicates not present)\
	stem4_DBH_cm		diameter at breast height for fourth stem in cm (NA indicates not present)	stem5_DBH_cm		diameter at breast height for fifth stem in cm (NA indicates not present)	stem6_DBH_cm		diameter at breast height for sixth stem in cm (NA indicates not present)\
	stem7_DBH_cm		diameter at breast height for seventh stem in cm (NA indicates not present)\
	stem8_DBH_cm		diameter at breast height for eighth stem in cm (NA indicates not present)\
	\
************************************************************************************************************\
\
3) \'93EdaphicAndSuccessionalData_WerdenEtAl2018_FunctionalEcology.csv\'94\
\
This file contains stand age estimates and edaphic variables for the 84 forest inventory plots collected by Powers et al. (2009) and Becknell and Powers (2014). Successional age in years (standAgeEstimate_yr) of each plot was determined using local knowledge and remote sensing (Powers et al. 2009) and are expressed relative to 2007. For edaphic variables, particle size distribution (sand, silt, and clay %) was determined with the hydrometer method (Bouyoucos 1962), total rock-derived nutrients (Al, B, Ca, Cr, Cu, Fe, K, Mg, Mn, Na, Ni, P, Zn) were determined with ICP-AES following hot nitric acid digestion at the Research Analytical Laboratory at the University of Minnesota, and total soil N (% oven dry) was determined using an elemental analyzer (Costech Analytical Technologies Inc., Valencia, CA) at the University of California, Davis. Please see the materials and methods section of our paper and Powers et al. (2009) for a detailed description of sample collection and analysis methods.\
\
The rows in this file are the 84 forest inventory plots and the columns are the edaphic and stand age data for each plot. We used a subset of these data for our analyses. For edaphic variables we used soil particle size distribution (% sand, silt, and clay), total Ca, K, Mg, Na, P (mg kg-1), and total C and N (%). We used the estimate of stand age estimate (standAgeEstimate_yr) as a metric of plot successional age. \
\
Variable names and column descriptions: \
\
	plotID				plot number (84 - 20 x 50 m plots total) 	\
	standAgeEstimate_yr	estimate of stand age in years\
	stoneContent_g_cm^-3	soil stone content (g cm^-3)\
	bulkDensity_g_cm^-3	soil bulk density (g cm^-3)\
	pH				soil pH\
	sand_%			soil sand percentage\
	silt_%				soil silt percentage\
	clay_%			soil clay percentage\
	deltaN15_perMil		soil \uc0\u948 15N (\'89)\
	delta13C_perMil		soil \uc0\u948 13C (\'89)	\
	Al_mg_kg^-1			total soil aluminum (mg kg^-1)\
	B_mg_kg^-1			total soil boron (mg kg^-1)\
	Ca_mg_kg^-1		total soil calcium (mg kg^-1)\
	Cr_mg_kg^-1			total soil chromium (mg kg^-1)\
	Cu_mg_kg^-1 		total soil copper (mg kg^-1)\
	Fe_mg_kg^-1 		total soil iron (mg kg^-1)\
	K_mg_kg^-1 			total soil potassium (mg kg^-1)\
	Mg_mg_kg^-1 		total soil magnesium (mg kg^-1)\
	Mn_mg_kg^-1 		total soil manganese (mg kg^-1)\
	Na_mg_kg^-1 		total soil sodium (mg kg^-1)	\
	Ni_mg_kg^-1 		total soil nickel (mg kg^-1)\
	P_mg_kg^-1 			total soil phosphorus (mg kg^-1)\
	Zn_mg_kg^-1 		total soil zinc (mg kg^-1)\
	N_% 				total soil nitrogen (%)\
	C_% 				total soil carbon (%)\
\
